{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>To the land of the Canaanite, etc.</b> And even though He counted only five nations, all seven nations are implied, for they are all included in Canaan — and one of the families of Canaan was not called by any name but Canaanite." "<b>Which He swore to your fathers.</b> To Abraham He says: ״בַּיּוֹם הַהוּא כָרַת ה' אֶת אַבְרָם וגו׳״ (Genesis 15); to Isaac He says: ״גּוּר בָאָרֶץ הַזֹּאת״ (ibid. 26); to Jacob He says: ״הָאָרֶץ אֲשֶׁר אַתָּה שֹׁכֵב עָלֶיהָ״ (ibid. 28)." "<b>Flowing with milk and honey.</b> Milk flows from the goats, and the honey flows from the dates and from the figs." "<b>This service.</b> Of the pesach. But was it not already stated above, ״וְהָיָה כִּי תָבֹאוּ אֶל הָאָרֶץ וגו׳״? Why did He repeat and state it? Because of something newly introduced in it: in the first passage it says ״וְהָיָה כִּי יֹאמְרוּ אֲלֵיכֶם בְנֵיכֶם מָה הָעֲבֹדָה הַזֹּאת לָכֶם״ — the verse speaks of the wicked son, who has removed himself from the collective; and here, ״וְהִגַּדְתָּ לְבִנְךָ״ — of the son who does not know how to ask, and Scripture teaches you that you must open for him with aggadah that draws the heart (Mekhilta)."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:51:59.323299163Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}